\b Capacity Dashboard Deployment Guide\b0\par
\i Word-friendly operator guide for Bicep and Terraform deployments with existing-resource reuse and management-group-first RBAC\i0\par
\par
This guide is written for someone who is comfortable following a checklist, even if they do not work with Azure deployments every day. It explains the safest order to do the work:\par
\par
1. Install the tools and collect the required values.\par
2. Deploy the Azure infrastructure and application.\par
3. Run the database step second, after the web app exists.\par
4. Verify sign-in, data access, and exports.\par
\par
\b 1. What This Deployment Creates\b0\par
\par
Both the Bicep and Terraform paths create the same main Azure resources by default:\par
\par
- Web App for the dashboard\par
- Function App for the worker\par
- Storage account for the worker\par
- Azure SQL Server and Azure SQL Database\par
- Key Vault\par
- Virtual network, private endpoints, and DNS links\par
- Application Insights and Log Analytics\par
- Managed identities and the main RBAC assignments\par
\par
Both paths can also attach to existing customer-managed shared services instead of creating duplicates for:\par
\par
- Azure SQL Server and optionally the Azure SQL Database\par
- Azure Key Vault\par
- Worker host Storage Account\par
- Virtual Network and the required App Service integration and private endpoint subnets\par
\par
\b 2. Safest Deployment Order\b0\par
\par
Use this order unless your customer has a very specific runbook:\par
\par
1. Start with \f1 scripts/Start-CapacityDeployment.ps1\f0 for a guided install, or run \f1 scripts/Start-CapacityDeployment.ps1 -PlanOnly\f0 to preview the generated \f1 deploy-infra.ps1\f0 command without starting deployment.\par
2. Prepare access and collect values.\par
3. Deploy infrastructure and application.\par
4. Run the database initialization step.\par
5. Test sign-in and the main dashboard pages.\par
\par
Do not try to start with the database scripts unless the web app and identities already exist. The current deployment flow expects the application and identities to be created first.\par
\par
\b 3. Before You Start\b0\par
\par
You will need these items before either deployment method:\par
\par
- An Azure subscription where you can create resources\par
- Azure permissions: Contributor and User Access Administrator on the target scope\par
- An Entra user or group that will be the Azure SQL Entra admin\par
- The Azure subscription ID\par
- A resource group name to use\par
- An environment name such as dev or test\par
- A workload suffix such as demo001\par
- Ingest API key. Terraform can generate one when omitted; Bicep deployments should supply it through the wrapper or parameter file.\par
- Session secret. Terraform can generate one when omitted; Bicep deployments should supply it through the wrapper or parameter file. Keep the same value on redeploy unless intentionally rotating sessions.\par
- Optional worker shared secret\par
- Optional Entra app registration values if sign-in should be enabled\par
- Optional Entra access group IDs. If you use the wrapper script and do not pass group IDs, it reuses existing \f1 CapacityAdmin\f0 and \f1 CapacityReportViewers\f0 groups by display name when auth is enabled. Missing groups stop the deployment unless the operator explicitly passes \f1 -CreateMissingEntraAccessGroups $true\f0.\par
- Preferred management group names if the tenant uses management groups\par
- Optional explicit subscription lists for fallback RBAC when management groups are not used\par
\par
\b 4. Install The Required Tools\b0\par
\par
Install these tools on the machine that will run the deployment:\par
\par
- Azure CLI\par
- PowerShell 7\par
- For Bicep: Azure CLI with Bicep support\par
- For Terraform: Terraform 1.5.0 or newer\par
- For manual database initialization: sqlcmd\par
\par
Recommended checks:\par
\par
- Open PowerShell and run \f1 az --version\f0\par
- Run \f1 pwsh --version\f0\par
- If using Terraform, run \f1 terraform version\f0\par
- If using manual SQL initialization, run \f1 sqlcmd -?\f0\par
\par
\b 5. Decide Which Path To Use\b0\par
\par
\b Use Bicep when:\b0\par
- You want the Azure-native path\par
- You want the simplest script-driven operator flow\par
- You do not need Terraform state management\par
\par
\b Use Terraform when:\b0\par
- The customer standard is Terraform\par
- You want Terraform state and plan/apply workflow\par
- You may need to import an existing resource group before first apply\par
\par
\b 6. Gather The Values To Fill In\b0\par
\par
Replace the placeholders in the commands later in this document with your own values:\par
\par
- \f1 <subscription-id>\f0\par
- \f1 <resource-group-name>\f0\par
- \f1 <environment>\f0 such as \f1 dev\f0 or \f1 test\f0\par
- \f1 <workload-suffix>\f0 such as \f1 demo001\f0\par
- \f1 <entra-upn>\f0 for the Azure SQL Entra admin login\par
- \f1 <entra-object-id>\f0 for the Azure SQL Entra admin object ID\par
- \f1 <tenant-id>\f0 if dashboard sign-in is enabled\par
- \f1 <client-id>\f0 if dashboard sign-in is enabled\par
- \f1 <client-secret>\f0 if dashboard sign-in is enabled\par
- \f1 <admin-group-object-id>\f0 if admin access is group-gated and the group is centrally managed\par
- \f1 <report-viewer-group-object-id>\f0 if report access is group-gated and the group is centrally managed\par
- \f1 <management-group-name>\f0 if management-group-based RBAC will be used\par
\par
\b 6A. What Those Secrets Actually Are\b0\par
\par
The guide mentions several application secrets. These are stored in Key Vault for deployed environments and then referenced by App Service app settings. Terraform can generate the ingest API key and session secret when omitted; the Entra client secret comes from the customer-created app registration.\par
\par
\b Entra client secret\b0\par
- Key Vault secret name: \f1 capdash-entra-client-secret\f0\par
- App setting: \f1 ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET\f0\par
- This is the Microsoft Entra app registration client secret used by the dashboard OAuth sign-in flow.\par
- It is required when \f1 AUTH_ENABLED=true\f0 and the dashboard should require Entra sign-in.\par
\par
\b Ingest API key\b0\par
- Key Vault secret name: \f1 capdash-ingest-api-key\f0\par
- App setting: \f1 INGEST_API_KEY\f0\par
- This is a shared secret used to protect the dashboard's internal ingestion and internal database/bootstrap endpoints.\par
- The server checks it through the \f1 INGEST_API_KEY\f0 app setting.\par
- Terraform can generate this value when \f1 ingest_api_key\f0 is omitted. Bicep deployments should supply it through parameters or the wrapper script.\par
- Think of it as an internal service password, not as an Azure-generated key.\par
\par
\b Session secret\b0\par
- Key Vault secret name: \f1 capdash-session-secret\f0\par
- App setting: \f1 SESSION_SECRET\f0\par
- This is the secret used by the web app's session cookie middleware.\par
- The server reads it from \f1 SESSION_SECRET\f0.\par
- Terraform can generate this value when \f1 session_secret\f0 is omitted. Bicep deployments should supply it through parameters or the wrapper script.\par
- If sign-in is enabled, this value is important because it protects the signed session cookie state.\par
- Keep it stable across redeploys unless intentionally rotating sessions, otherwise existing sessions are invalidated.\par
- The helper script \f1 scripts/New-EntraApp.ps1\f0 already prints an example generated \f1 SESSION_SECRET\f0 for local setup.\par
\par
\b Optional worker shared secret\b0\par
- This is a shared secret between the web app and the worker app.\par
- The web app receives it as \f1 CAPACITY_WORKER_SHARED_SECRET\f0 and the worker app receives it as \f1 WORKER_SHARED_SECRET\f0.\par
- You only need this when you are using the shared-secret web-to-worker contract.\par
- You create this value yourself the same way: make it a long random string and pass the same value to both sides through deployment.\par
- It does not come from Azure Key Vault automatically and it is not auto-generated by the deployment template.\par
\par
\b Simple rule:\b0\par
- If you are doing a Terraform deployment, you may omit \f1 ingest_api_key\f0 and \f1 session_secret\f0 and let Terraform generate and store them in Key Vault. Secure the Terraform state because generated sensitive values are stored there.\par
- If you are doing a Bicep deployment or intentionally need fixed known values, generate these values yourself and store them securely.\par
- If you are redeploying an existing environment, reuse the same values from that environment unless you intentionally want to rotate them.\par
\par
\b Easy way to generate random values in PowerShell\b0\par
\par
\f1 [Convert]::ToBase64String([System.Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::GetBytes(32))\f0\par
\par
Use one generated value for \f1 INGEST_API_KEY\f0, a different one for \f1 SESSION_SECRET\f0, and optionally a third one for \f1 WORKER_SHARED_SECRET\f0 when you choose to supply those values manually. Do not reuse the same string for all three.\par
\par
\b 7. Existing Resource Reuse and RBAC Strategy\b0\par
\par
This is the most important section for non-standard customer environments.\par
\par
\b Supported directly by the current deployment inputs:\b0\par
- Existing resource group with Terraform, as long as you import it before first apply\par
- Different resource group region with Terraform, using \f1 resource_group_location\f0\par
- Key Vault naming conflict with Terraform, using \f1 key_vault_name_override\f0\par
- Reusing an existing Azure SQL server with \f1 ExistingSqlServerName\f0 or \f1 existing_sql_server_name\f0\par
- Reusing an existing Azure SQL database with \f1 ExistingSqlDatabaseName\f0 or \f1 existing_sql_database_name\f0\par
- Reusing an existing Key Vault with \f1 ExistingKeyVaultName\f0 or \f1 existing_key_vault_name\f0\par
- Reusing an existing worker storage account with \f1 ExistingWorkerStorageAccountName\f0 or \f1 existing_worker_storage_account_name\f0\par
- Reusing an existing Virtual Network with \f1 ExistingVirtualNetworkName\f0 or \f1 existing_virtual_network_name\f0, plus the two required subnet names\par
- Manual database initialization against customer-provided SQL names after the app is deployed\par
\par
\b App Service naming and package publish behavior:\b0\par
- The deployment wrapper checks the requested Web App and Function App host names before deploying.\par
- If \f1 app-capdash-<environment>-<workload-suffix>\f0 or \f1 func-capdash-<environment>-<workload-suffix>-appsvc\f0 is already globally reserved outside the target resource group, the wrapper generates a randomized effective workload suffix and uses that same suffix for infrastructure, web package deployment, worker deployment, and database bootstrap.\par
- After Bicep or Terraform finishes, the wrapper reads the actual Web App and Function App names from deployment outputs before publishing packages. This avoids deploying to a guessed name.\par
- Raw Bicep or raw Terraform creates infrastructure only. Use \f1 scripts/deploy-infra.ps1\f0 when you want the dashboard web package and worker package deployed automatically.\par
\par
\b Entra access group behavior:\b0\par
- Raw Bicep and raw Terraform accept group object IDs but do not create Entra groups.\par
- The wrapper script reuses existing \f1 CapacityAdmin\f0 and \f1 CapacityReportViewers\f0 groups when \f1 -AuthEnabled $true\f0 is used and explicit group IDs are omitted.\par
- Missing groups stop the deployment by default so the dashboard does not accidentally deploy with broad authenticated-user access.\par
- If the customer requires centrally managed groups, pre-create the groups and pass \f1 -AdminGroupId\f0 and \f1 -ReportViewerGroupIds\f0. Use \f1 -CreateMissingEntraAccessGroups $true\f0 only when the deployment identity is allowed to create Entra security groups.\par
\par
\b Important behavior when reusing shared services:\b0\par
- Existing resource names are the switch. If you provide the existing resource name, deployment moves that dependency into reuse mode. Use names, not full Azure resource IDs.\par
- \f1 ExistingSqlDatabaseName\f0 requires \f1 ExistingSqlServerName\f0 because the reused database must hang off a reused server.\par
- When you reuse an existing SQL server or Key Vault, the templates do not create a new private endpoint or private DNS path for that dependency. They assume the customer-managed private connectivity path already exists.\par
- When you reuse an existing Virtual Network, the templates do not create or modify the VNet or its subnets. The customer must pre-create one subnet delegated to \f1 Microsoft.Web/serverFarms\f0 for Web App and Function App VNet integration, and one subnet that allows private endpoints for newly-created SQL or Key Vault private endpoints.\par
- If the reused dependency lives in a different resource group, use the matching resource-group override input.\par
\par
\b Preferred RBAC approach for larger estates:\b0\par
1. Use management-group inputs first so access inherits to descendant subscriptions.\par
2. Use explicit subscription lists only as the fallback path for smaller estates or tightly curated environments.\par
3. Set \f1 quota_management_group_id\f0 or \f1 -QuotaManagementGroupId\f0 when Quota Workbench should default to a known management group or when tenant-wide management-group enumeration is restricted.\par
\par
The key roles that can be assigned through deployment are:\par
\par
- Web App identity: \f1 Reader\f0 for subscription discovery and read-only ARM queries\par
- Web App identity: \f1 GroupQuota Request Operator\f0 for quota apply and movement workflows\par
- Worker identity: \f1 Compute Recommendations Role\f0 for capacity recommendation APIs\par
- Worker identity: \f1 Cost Management Reader\f0 for pricing and cost lookups\par
- Worker identity: \f1 Billing Reader\f0 for billing meter and offer data\par
\par
The safest current approach is:\par
\par
1. Deploy the application and supporting resources first.\par
2. Disable automatic database bootstrap if needed.\par
3. Run the manual database initialization command against the effective SQL server and database names.\par
4. Document any extra secret, DNS, firewall, or RBAC mapping needed for that customer.\par
\par
\b 8. Bicep Runbook\b0\par
\par
\b Recommended Bicep command:\b0\par
\par
\f1 .\scripts\deploy-infra.ps1 `\par
-ResourceGroupName "<resource-group-name>" `\par
-Environment <environment> `\par
-WorkloadSuffix "<workload-suffix>" `\par
-ParameterFile "./infra/bicep/main.bicepparam" `\par
-SqlEntraAdminLogin "<entra-upn>" `\par
-SqlEntraAdminObjectId "<entra-object-id>" `\par
-SubscriptionId "<subscription-id>"\f0\par
\par
\b Add these only when needed:\b0\par
- \f1 -QuotaManagementGroupId "<management-group-id>"\f0\par
- \f1 -WebReaderManagementGroupNames @("<management-group-name>")\f0\par
- \f1 -WebQuotaWriterManagementGroupNames @("<management-group-name>")\f0\par
- \f1 -WorkerRbacManagementGroupNames @("<management-group-name>")\f0\par
- \f1 -UseAllAccessibleManagementGroups\f0 if the operator should use every non-root management group they can see\par
- \f1 -AuthEnabled $true\f0 plus tenant, client, and secret values if sign-in should be enabled. This is already the deployment default now. If group IDs are omitted, the wrapper reuses existing \f1 CapacityAdmin\f0 and \f1 CapacityReportViewers\f0 groups by display name.\par
- \f1 -AdminGroupId "<admin-group-object-id>"\f0 and \f1 -ReportViewerGroupIds "<report-viewer-group-object-id>"\f0 when the customer already has approved groups\par
- \f1 -CreateMissingEntraAccessGroups $true\f0 only when the deployment identity is allowed to create missing Entra security groups\par
- \f1 -RandomizeWorkloadSuffixOnNameConflict $false\f0 when the deployment should fail fast instead of generating a randomized suffix for App Service host-name conflicts\par
- \f1 -ExistingSqlServerName "<sql-server-name>"\f0 to reuse an existing SQL server\par
- \f1 -ExistingSqlServerResourceGroupName "<sql-rg-name>"\f0 when the existing SQL server is in a different resource group\par
- \f1 -ExistingSqlDatabaseName "<sql-database-name>"\f0 to reuse an existing SQL database on that server\par
- \f1 -ExistingKeyVaultName "<key-vault-name>"\f0 to reuse an existing Key Vault\par
- \f1 -ExistingKeyVaultResourceGroupName "<key-vault-rg-name>"\f0 when the existing Key Vault is in a different resource group\par
- \f1 -ExistingWorkerStorageAccountName "<storage-account-name>"\f0 to reuse an existing worker storage account\par
- \f1 -ExistingWorkerStorageResourceGroupName "<storage-rg-name>"\f0 when the existing worker storage account is in a different resource group\par
- \f1 -ExistingVirtualNetworkName "<vnet-name>"\f0 to reuse an existing Virtual Network\par
- \f1 -ExistingVirtualNetworkResourceGroupName "<network-rg-name>"\f0 when the existing Virtual Network is in a different resource group\par
- \f1 -ExistingAppServiceIntegrationSubnetName "<delegated-subnet-name>"\f0 for the subnet delegated to \f1 Microsoft.Web/serverFarms\f0\par
- \f1 -ExistingPrivateEndpointSubnetName "<private-endpoint-subnet-name>"\f0 for SQL and Key Vault private endpoints created by the deployment\par
- \f1 -ApplyDatabaseBootstrap $false\f0 if the database must be initialized separately\par
\par
\b Bicep example: reuse customer-owned network, Key Vault, storage, and SQL\b0\par
\par
Use this pattern when the customer wants the deployment to create the dashboard apps and supporting runtime settings, but attach to shared resources they already own. Replace every placeholder with the customer-provided name.\par
\par
\f1 .\scripts\deploy-infra.ps1 `\par
-ResourceGroupName "<resource-group-name>" `\par
-Environment <environment> `\par
-WorkloadSuffix "<workload-suffix>" `\par
-ParameterFile "./infra/bicep/main.bicepparam" `\par
-SqlEntraAdminLogin "<entra-upn>" `\par
-SqlEntraAdminObjectId "<entra-object-id>" `\par
-SubscriptionId "<subscription-id>" `\par
-ExistingVirtualNetworkName "<vnet-name>" `\par
-ExistingVirtualNetworkResourceGroupName "<network-rg-name>" `\par
-ExistingAppServiceIntegrationSubnetName "<delegated-appservice-subnet-name>" `\par
-ExistingPrivateEndpointSubnetName "<private-endpoint-subnet-name>" `\par
-ExistingKeyVaultName "<key-vault-name>" `\par
-ExistingKeyVaultResourceGroupName "<key-vault-rg-name>" `\par
-ExistingWorkerStorageAccountName "<storage-account-name>" `\par
-ExistingWorkerStorageResourceGroupName "<storage-rg-name>" `\par
-ExistingSqlServerName "<sql-server-name>" `\par
-ExistingSqlServerResourceGroupName "<sql-rg-name>" `\par
-ExistingSqlDatabaseName "<sql-database-name>" `\par
-QuotaManagementGroupId "<management-group-name>" `\par
-WebReaderManagementGroupNames @("<management-group-name>") `\par
-WebQuotaWriterManagementGroupNames @("<management-group-name>") `\par
-WorkerRbacManagementGroupNames @("<management-group-name>")\f0\par
\par
If the customer only wants to reuse some resources, remove the unused existing-resource lines. For example, if the customer only provides an existing VNet and storage account, omit the existing SQL and Key Vault lines so Bicep creates those resources.\par
\par
\b When to use test.bicepparam:\b0\par
Use \f1 ./infra/bicep/test.bicepparam\f0 when you are building a stable demo or test environment instead of a change-heavy dev environment.\par
\par
\b 9. Terraform Runbook\b0\par
\par
\b Step 1: Create a local tfvars file\b0\par
Copy \f1 infra/terraform/terraform.tfvars.example\f0 to \f1 infra/terraform/terraform.tfvars\f0 and fill in the real values.\par
Do not commit \f1 terraform.tfvars\f0 because it can contain tenant IDs, app registration secrets, and customer resource names.\par
\par
\b Terraform tfvars example: create app resources but reuse customer-owned network, Key Vault, storage, and SQL\b0\par
\par
Use this pattern when the customer wants to bypass creating shared dependencies and reuse their existing resources by name. Terraform still creates the dashboard Web App, Function App, managed identities, app settings, and requested RBAC assignments.\par
\par
\f1 resource_group_name     = "<resource-group-name>"\par
resource_group_location = "<resource-group-location>"\par
location                = "<resource-location>"\par
environment             = "<environment>"\par
workload_suffix         = "<workload-suffix>"\par
\par
sql_entra_admin_login     = "<entra-upn>"\par
sql_entra_admin_object_id = "<entra-object-id>"\par
\par
# Existing customer-managed network. These names make Terraform skip VNet and subnet creation.\par
existing_virtual_network_name                  = "<vnet-name>"\par
existing_virtual_network_resource_group_name   = "<network-rg-name>"\par
existing_app_service_integration_subnet_name   = "<delegated-appservice-subnet-name>"\par
existing_private_endpoint_subnet_name          = "<private-endpoint-subnet-name>"\par
\par
# Existing customer-managed Key Vault. Omit these lines if Terraform should create a new Key Vault.\par
existing_key_vault_name                = "<key-vault-name>"\par
existing_key_vault_resource_group_name = "<key-vault-rg-name>"\par
\par
# Existing customer-managed worker storage account. Omit these lines if Terraform should create storage.\par
existing_worker_storage_account_name                = "<storage-account-name>"\par
existing_worker_storage_account_resource_group_name = "<storage-rg-name>"\par
\par
# Existing customer-managed SQL. Omit these lines if Terraform should create SQL server and database.\par
existing_sql_server_name                = "<sql-server-name>"\par
existing_sql_server_resource_group_name = "<sql-rg-name>"\par
existing_sql_database_name              = "<sql-database-name>"\par
\par
# Auth defaults to enabled. Terraform can manage the generated callback URI if the deploy identity can update the app registration.\par
auth_enabled                  = true\par
entra_tenant_id               = "<tenant-id>"\par
entra_client_id               = "<client-id>"\par
entra_client_secret           = "<client-secret>"\par
manage_entra_web_redirect_uri = true\par
\par
# Management-group-first RBAC and subscription discovery.\par
quota_management_group_id                 = "<management-group-name>"\par
web_reader_management_group_names         = ["<management-group-name>"]\par
web_quota_writer_management_group_names   = ["<management-group-name>"]\par
worker_rbac_management_group_names        = ["<management-group-name>"]\f0\par
\par
If the customer only wants to reuse some dependencies, remove the unused existing-resource lines. Empty strings and empty arrays are not required in \f1 terraform.tfvars\f0; omitted values keep the Terraform defaults.\par
\par
\b Step 2: If the resource group already exists, import it before first apply\b0\par
\par
\f1 terraform import -var-file="terraform.tfvars" azurerm_resource_group.rg "/subscriptions/<subscription-id>/resourceGroups/<resource-group-name>"\f0\par
\par
\b Step 3: Run Terraform from the wrapper script or directly\b0\par
\par
\f1 .\scripts\deploy-infra.ps1 `\par
-Provider Terraform `\par
-ResourceGroupName "<resource-group-name>" `\par
-Environment <environment> `\par
-WorkloadSuffix "<workload-suffix>" `\par
-SqlEntraAdminLogin "<entra-upn>" `\par
-SqlEntraAdminObjectId "<entra-object-id>" `\par
-SubscriptionId "<subscription-id>"\f0\par
\par
Or use raw Terraform:\par
\par
\f1 cd infra/terraform\par
terraform init\par
terraform apply -var-file="terraform.tfvars"\f0\par
\par
\b Helpful Terraform-specific extras:\b0\par
- \f1 resource_group_location\f0 if the existing resource group is in a different region\par
- \f1 key_vault_name_override\f0 if the default Key Vault name cannot be used\par
- \f1 manage_entra_web_redirect_uri = true\f0 if Terraform should add the generated callback URL to an existing Entra app registration\par
- \f1 existing_virtual_network_name\f0, \f1 existing_app_service_integration_subnet_name\f0, and \f1 existing_private_endpoint_subnet_name\f0 to reuse a customer-managed VNet and subnets\par
- \f1 existing_sql_server_name\f0, \f1 existing_sql_database_name\f0, \f1 existing_key_vault_name\f0, and \f1 existing_worker_storage_account_name\f0 to reuse customer-managed shared services\par
- \f1 web_reader_management_group_names\f0, \f1 web_quota_writer_management_group_names\f0, and \f1 worker_rbac_management_group_names\f0 for management-group-first RBAC\par
\par
When using \f1 scripts/deploy-infra.ps1 -Provider Terraform\f0, prefer putting optional customer-owned resource values in \f1 terraform.tfvars\f0. The wrapper now avoids sending empty optional values so it does not wipe tfvars entries such as \f1 web_reader_management_group_names\f0.\par
The Terraform wrapper also checks App Service host-name availability, randomizes the effective workload suffix on conflict, reuses existing \f1 CapacityAdmin\f0 and \f1 CapacityReportViewers\f0 groups when group IDs are omitted, and publishes the web and worker packages after apply. Raw \f1 terraform apply\f0 does not create Entra groups and does not publish application packages; run the wrapper or deploy the packages separately afterward. Missing groups stop the wrapper unless \f1 -CreateMissingEntraAccessGroups $true\f0 is explicitly supplied.\par
\par
\b 10. Database Step Second\b0\par
\par
The safest operator pattern is to do the database step after the deployment creates the app, identity, SQL server name, and database name.\par
\par
\b Default path:\b0\par
The deployment script attempts dashboard bootstrap through the deployed web app.\par
\par
\b Separate DBA or customer-managed path:\b0\par
Use \f1 -ApplyDatabaseBootstrap $false\f0 during deployment, then run the manual database command afterward.\par
\par
Standard example:\par
\par
\f1 .\scripts\initialize-database.ps1 -SqlServer "sql-capdash-<environment>-<workload-suffix>.database.windows.net" -SqlDatabase "sqldb-capdash-<environment>" -AppIdentityName "app-capdash-<environment>-<workload-suffix>"\f0\par
\par
If the customer pre-created SQL, substitute the actual server and database names.\par
\par
Run this from an approved network path when Azure SQL is private-only, such as:\par
\par
- An Azure VM with network access to the private endpoint\par
- A self-hosted runner inside the approved network\par
- An admin workstation that reaches Azure through the approved customer network path\par
\par
\b 11. When The Customer Already Has A Database\b0\par
\par
If the customer says, "We already have the SQL server and database," use this checklist:\par
\par
1. Confirm the real SQL server FQDN and database name.\par
2. Confirm who is the Azure SQL Entra admin.\par
3. Pass \f1 ExistingSqlServerName\f0 and optionally \f1 ExistingSqlDatabaseName\f0 during deployment.\par
4. Skip automatic database bootstrap if the customer wants a separate DBA step.\par
5. Run \f1 scripts/initialize-database.ps1\f0 with the customer SQL names if schema bootstrap still needs to be applied.\par
6. Confirm the app identity only keeps the intended runtime roles afterward.\par
\par
\b 12. When The Customer Already Has A Key Vault\b0\par
\par
The current templates now provide a direct reuse switch for an existing Key Vault.\par
\par
What is supported today:\par
\par
- If Terraform only needs a different vault name because the default name is blocked, use \f1 key_vault_name_override\f0.\par
- If the customer wants to reuse an existing Key Vault, use \f1 ExistingKeyVaultName\f0 or \f1 existing_key_vault_name\f0.\par
\par
What to still confirm when reusing a customer-owned Key Vault:\par
\par
1. Confirm the customer-managed private endpoint and DNS path already exists if the vault is private-only.\par
2. Confirm the web app and worker identities can receive \f1 Key Vault Secrets User\f0 on that vault.\par
3. Confirm the expected secrets live in that vault and use the expected names.\par
\par
\b 13. When The Customer Already Has A Storage Account\b0\par
\par
The current deployment can reuse a customer-owned worker storage account through \f1 ExistingWorkerStorageAccountName\f0 or \f1 existing_worker_storage_account_name\f0.\par
\par
What to still confirm:\par
\par
1. The worker managed identity can receive the required storage data-plane roles.\par
2. The storage account is appropriate for Azure Functions host storage.\par
3. The account lives in the expected resource group, or you pass the resource-group override input.\par
\par
\b 14. When The Customer Already Has A Virtual Network\b0\par
\par
The current deployment can reuse a customer-owned Virtual Network through \f1 ExistingVirtualNetworkName\f0 or \f1 existing_virtual_network_name\f0. This bypasses VNet and subnet creation.\par
\par
What to confirm before deployment:\par
\par
1. The VNet name and resource group are correct.\par
2. The App Service integration subnet already exists and is delegated to \f1 Microsoft.Web/serverFarms\f0.\par
3. The private endpoint subnet already exists and can host private endpoints for SQL and Key Vault when those resources are created by the deployment.\par
4. Private DNS and routing match the customer network design, especially when SQL or Key Vault are also customer-owned and private-only.\par
5. If SQL, Key Vault, or storage are reused in a different resource group, pass each matching resource-group override input.\par
\par
Do not pass full subnet or VNet resource IDs. Use the VNet name, VNet resource group name, App Service integration subnet name, and private endpoint subnet name.\par
\par
\b 15. Basic Validation Checklist\b0\par
\par
After deployment and database initialization, check these items:\par
\par
- The web app is reachable in a browser\par
- The deployment output or console log shows the effective Web App name. If the workload suffix was randomized, use the generated name for browser testing and app registration callback checks.\par
- The sign-in page works if auth is enabled\par
- \f1 CapacityAdmin\f0 and \f1 CapacityReportViewers\f0 exist, or the approved customer group IDs were passed explicitly\par
- The dashboard loads after sign-in\par
- The main grid loads data\par
- Exports work after sign-in\par
- The worker app is deployed\par
- The SQL database is initialized\par
- Key Vault references resolve\par
- Management-group or subscription RBAC is in place\par
- \f1 INGEST_MANAGEMENT_GROUP_NAMES\f0 is populated when management-group subscription discovery is expected\par
- Quota Workbench can load its management-group default or fallback without a blank page\par
- Existing-resource reuse settings match the intended SQL, Key Vault, worker storage, and VNet dependencies\par
\par
\b 16. Simple Decision Guide\b0\par
\par
If the person following this guide wants the least surprising path, tell them to do this:\par
\par
1. Choose Bicep unless the customer requires Terraform.\par
2. Use the deployment script, not raw template commands.\par
3. Treat the database step as second, not first.\par
4. Let the wrapper randomize the workload suffix on App Service host-name conflicts unless the customer requires deterministic names.\par
5. If the customer already owns SQL, Key Vault, storage, or VNet resources, use the existing-resource inputs and verify private connectivity, subnet delegation, DNS, and RBAC assumptions before deployment.\par
